title: Provider searches have no timeout - one stalled provider blocks search() in
  collect mode forever
updated: 2026-10-15T04:48:01Z
---

The only timeout in the distribution is `fetch_timeout` for the optional page fetch (`lib/Net/Async/WebSearch.pm:156-157, 206`). The provider requests themselves carry none: `grep -rn timeout lib/Net/Async/WebSearch/Provider.pm lib/Net/Async/WebSearch/Provider/` is empty, and the shared Net::Async::HTTP is built without one (`WebSearch.pm:91-95`). `search` in the default `collect` mode waits for all providers with `Future->needs_all(@wrapped)` (`WebSearch.pm:286`); a provider failure is converted into an error entry, but a provider that accepts the connection and never answers produces no failure at all.

Effect: with 11 providers behind third-party endpoints, one hanging endpoint keeps the whole aggregated search pending indefinitely, although the results of the others are already there.

Done when: there is a per-provider timeout (constructor default, overridable per `search` call) applied to every provider request, a timed-out provider shows up in `errors` like any other failure, and a test with a never-resolving mock http covers it.

